Now I see it! The critical change in commit `4bc8660`:

**Before (old `registerBackgroundEventHandler`):**
```js
notifee.onBackgroundEvent(async (event) => {
  if (event.type === EventType.PRESS) {
    if (!NavigationContainerRef?.isReady()) return;  // ← RETURNS EARLY if nav not ready
    const onSplash = NavigationContainerRef.getCurrentRoute()?.name === "splash";
    await this.handleNotificationPress(event.detail.notification, onSplash);


```

**After (new `registerBackgroundEventHandler`):**
```js
notifee.onBackgroundEvent(async (event) => {
  if (event.type === EventType.PRESS) {
    await this.handleNotificationPress(
      event.detail.notification,
      isInitialNotificationContext(),
    );
  }
});
```

The key difference is that instead of returning early when the navigation container isn't ready, the new version extracts the logic into a helper function that returns `true` when navigation isn't ready (treating it as an initial context) or checks if we're on the splash screen. This ensures the notification handler always executes rather than silently failing.

When the app is killed and a notification is tapped, the old code would skip processing entirely and rely on the splash screen to catch it later. The new approach immediately calls the handler with the initial context flag, which should make cold-start notifications more reliable. But I need to think through what happens when the app is backgrounded but still running — in that case the navigation container is ready, so `isInitialNotificationContext` would check the current route instead.

